Subject: [PATCH 17/18] amd64: enumerate PCI, so the bus below the firmware exists

CONFIG_DRIVER_PCI has been `true` in config/examples/amd64_config.xml since the
port started. drivers/pci.c was compiled, linked, and NEVER CALLED: arch/amd64's
arch_init() had no ob_pci_init(), so amd64 had no host bridge, no PCI children,
and nothing downstream of the bus could run.

Measured at the amd64 prompt, before and after:

                                   before          after
  dev /pci8086,1237@0              no such device  5 children
  openbios-video-width             0               320 (hex; 800 decimal)
  Cannot manage 'ISA bridge' ...   absent          present, as on x86

The tree gains what x86 has had all along -- pci8086,7000@0 (ISA), 7010@0 (IDE),
7113@0 (ACPI), QEMU,VGA@0 and e1000@0 -- and the `Cannot manage '<class>'` lines
appear in the boot log for the first time on this arch. They are not errors: PCI
enumeration prints one per device it has no driver for, and x86 has always
printed the same five.

The `arch = &default_pci_host` + `" /" open-dev to my-self` preamble is copied
from arch/x86/openbios.c because amd64 boots the SAME QEMU machine (`-M pc`,
i440FX). ob_pci_init() walks the bus with new-device/finish-device and needs a
current instance to hang the bridge off; without it the first new-device faults.

THREE THINGS THIS PATCH DOES NOT DO, each measured rather than assumed:

1. It does not move IDE "onto the probed path". TODO 13.1 said it should, and
   TODO 13.1 was wrong: drivers/ide.c:1519 takes `const char *path` and NEVER
   READS IT AGAIN. ob_ide_init() calls `new-device` in whatever device context
   happens to be current, which is root -- so `dev / ls` shows ide@0..ide@3 at
   the TOP LEVEL on x86 too, and there is no /pci node on either arch. The
   string "/pci/pci-ata" vs x86's "/pci/isa" has never meant anything. Left
   as-is, with a comment, so this stays a diff about PCI.

2. It does not deduplicate the IDE nodes. Enumeration now finds 8086:7010 and
   reports it unmanaged; ob_ide_init() still creates its own ide@N from the
   legacy ports. That is exactly what x86 does, and amd64-linux still boots off
   ide@1, so the duplication is upstream behaviour and not something this patch
   introduced.

3. include/arch/amd64/io.h gains `#include "asm/types.h"`, which x86's copy has
   always had. Turning DRIVER_VGA on pulls in drivers/vga_load_regs.c, whose
   include chain reaches include/drivers/vga.h -- and THAT header declares
   `extern volatile uint32_t *dac;` without including a types header at all. It
   built on x86 by accident, through asm/io.h. Fixing include/drivers/vga.h
   would be the more correct repair, but it is a SHARED header and would need
   the ppc and sparc arms; this restores parity inside the arch's own include
   dir, which is where the divergence is.

CONFIG_DRIVER_VGA flips to true here because with enumeration wired up it is now
reachable -- see patch 18 for why reachable was not yet the same as working.

All eleven smoke tracks pass after this patch, on all three arches this lab
drives: multiboot, dict-identity, nvram, amd64, amd64-fault, amd64-ctx,
amd64-pmem, amd64-linux, ppc, floppy, property-abi.

--- a/arch/amd64/openbios.c
+++ b/arch/amd64/openbios.c
@@ -33,6 +33,23 @@
 #define DICTIONARY_SIZE (256*1024)      /* 256K for the dictionary   */
 static char intdict[DICTIONARY_SIZE];
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_DRIVER_PCI
+/* The i440FX host bridge QEMU's `pc` machine presents, byte-identical to
+ * arch/x86/openbios.c:37. amd64 boots the SAME machine type -- the smoke
+ * tracks all run `-M pc` -- so a second, divergent copy would be a lie
+ * waiting to happen, not a port. */
+static const pci_arch_t default_pci_host = {
+    .name = "Intel,i440FX",
+    .vendor_id = PCI_VENDOR_ID_INTEL,
+    .device_id = PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_82441,
+    .io_base = 0x1000,
+    .host_ranges = {
+        { .type = IO_SPACE, .parentaddr = 0, .childaddr = 0x1000, .len = 0 },
+        { .type = 0, .parentaddr = 0, .childaddr = 0, .len = 0 }
+     }
+};
+#endif
+
 static void init_memory(void)
 {
 	/* push start and end of available memory to the stack
@@ -59,8 +76,37 @@
 	 * declared setup_timers() since a sweep amd64 was never compiled for. */
 
 	modules_init();
+#ifdef CONFIG_DRIVER_PCI
+	/* amd64 had NO PCI enumeration at all until here: CONFIG_DRIVER_PCI was
+	 * already true, so drivers/pci.c was compiled and linked in, and nothing
+	 * ever called it. Everything downstream of the bus was therefore
+	 * unreachable -- including vga_config_cb(), the ONLY caller of the
+	 * tree's only `byte-load`, which is why CONFIG_DRIVER_VGA could not be
+	 * turned on by flipping the flag.
+	 *
+	 * The `open-dev to my-self` dance is not decoration: ob_pci_init() walks
+	 * the bus with `new-device`/`finish-device` and needs a current instance
+	 * to hang the host bridge off, exactly as arch/x86/openbios.c does. */
+	arch = &default_pci_host;
+
+	push_str("/");
+	fword("find-device");
+	feval("\" /\" open-dev to my-self");
+
+	ob_pci_init();
+
+	feval("0 to my-self");
+#endif
 #ifdef CONFIG_DRIVER_IDE
 	setup_timers();
+	/* The path argument is DEAD. drivers/ide.c:1519 names it and never
+	 * reads it again -- ob_ide_init() calls `new-device` in whatever device
+	 * context is current, so the nodes land wherever that points (root, on
+	 * both arches: `dev / ls` shows ide@0..ide@3 at the top level on x86
+	 * too, and there is no /pci node on either). Left reading
+	 * "/pci/pci-ata" so this port stays a diff about PCI; TODO 13.1's
+	 * "move IDE onto the probed path" was written before the argument was
+	 * measured, and there is no probed path to move it to. */
 	ob_ide_init("/pci/pci-ata", 0x1f0, 0x3f6, 0x170, 0x376);
 #endif
 	/* P3: create /nvram and bind the config variables to it, exactly as
--- a/include/arch/amd64/io.h
+++ b/include/arch/amd64/io.h
@@ -1,6 +1,20 @@
 #ifndef _ASM_IO_H
 #define _ASM_IO_H
 
+/* include/arch/x86/io.h has had this line all along; amd64's copy is the odd
+ * one out, and nothing noticed because nothing amd64 compiled had needed a
+ * fixed-width type through this path. CONFIG_DRIVER_VGA pulls in
+ * drivers/vga_load_regs.c, whose include chain is asm/io.h -> drivers/vga.h,
+ * and drivers/vga.h declares `extern volatile uint32_t *dac;` WITHOUT
+ * including a types header -- so it built on x86 by accident and failed on
+ * amd64 with "unknown type name 'uint32_t'".
+ *
+ * Fixing drivers/vga.h instead would be the more correct repair (a header
+ * should include what it uses) but it is a SHARED header: that patch would
+ * need the ppc and sparc arms too. This restores parity inside the arch's own
+ * include dir, which is where the divergence actually is. */
+#include "asm/types.h"
+
 extern char _start, _end;
 extern unsigned long virt_offset;
 
--- a/config/examples/amd64_config.xml
+++ b/config/examples/amd64_config.xml
@@ -58,4 +58,4 @@
   <option name="CONFIG_DRIVER_IDE" type="boolean" value="true"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_IDE_NUM_CHANNELS" type="integer" value="4"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_DEBUG_IDE" type="boolean" value="false"/>
-  <option name="CONFIG_DRIVER_VGA" type="boolean" value="false"/>
+  <option name="CONFIG_DRIVER_VGA" type="boolean" value="true"/>
